    "translation": "Xenophon arrives at Sardis. There Cyrus and Proxenus are preparing the army. Xenophon comes to his friend; now the long journey begins.\n\nProxenus welcomes Xenophon and says: “Greetings, my friend. This commander is Cyrus.” Cyrus welcomes Xenophon and invites him to join the army.\n\nXenophon is neither a general nor a captain nor an ordinary soldier. He is present because of his friendship with Proxenus. He is a friend of a general, but he does not command the soldiers.\n\nMany Greeks are at Sardis. Xenias leads hoplites, while Proxenus has hoplites and light-armed men. Other generals lead their own contingents; they do not all come from the same country.\n\nXenophon sees the guards and the weapons. The guards train their bodies and protect the camp’s possessions. The brave protect not only themselves but also their friends.\n\nCyrus speaks about the Pisidians. Xenophon hears the account, but does not know the journey’s true objective. Friendship with Proxenus does not reveal everything.\n\nIn the camp Xenophon reflects: “I prepare myself. But the general does not look after only himself. He protects the others. Is a good general also a good guardian?”\n\nA separate account of Socrates concerns leadership. Socrates calls the general a shepherd of the soldiers. A shepherd keeps the sheep safe; a general protects the soldiers and provides food. Good people care for others.",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 1.2.1–5. https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Aabo%3Atlg%2C0032%2C006%3A1%3A2\nXenophon, Anabasis 3.1.4, 8–10. https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Aabo%3Atlg%2C0032%2C006%3A3\nXenophon, Memorabilia 3.2.1–4. https://monadnock.net/xenophon/mem3-2.html",
    "notesMarkdown": "Source and adaptation boundaries are stated in the introduction. All Greek is course-adapted present-tense narrative, not a verbatim ancient extract. Blue glosses support forms beyond the taught grammar; those untaught constructions are not assessed as morphology or production targets."
