  "culture": {
    "title": "The March of the Ten Thousand",
    "body": [
      "Xenophon’s Anabasis tells the story of Cyrus the Younger’s expedition and the Greek troops who served him. In 401 BCE Cyrus assembled an army at Sardis, claiming that he meant to campaign against the Pisidians. His real aim was to challenge his brother, Artaxerxes II, for the Persian throne. The army marched deep into Mesopotamia and fought the king’s forces at Cunaxa, where Cyrus was killed.",
      "After the Greek commanders were seized and killed, the soldiers chose new leaders, including Xenophon. They fought their way north through the mountains of Armenia, reached the Black Sea at Trapezus, and continued west. Xenophon’s account ends in western Asia Minor. Follow the journey on the animated map below; the route is a historical reconstruction, and some locations remain uncertain."
    ],
    "interactiveMap": {
      "src": "maps/ten-thousand.html",
      "title": "The March of the Ten Thousand animated route",
      "caption": "Play the expedition from Sardis to Cunaxa, the Black Sea, and Pergamum. Select a stop or change the playback speed to explore the route."
    },
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "An army gathered by personal networks",
        "body": [
          "Anabasis 1.2.1–5 presents Cyrus collecting forces from several sources. The Greeks at Sardis belong to distinct contingents with their own commanders; Proxenus brings heavy and light infantry. The army’s organization is more complex than a single city sending its citizen force. Cyrus’s announced target is the Pisidians, while Tissaphernes suspects preparations on a larger scale.",
          "Personal relationships make a practical difference. Anabasis 3.1.4 and 8–10 connects Xenophon’s arrival to Proxenus’s invitation and Cyrus’s welcome. Friendship provides access but does not automatically make the visitor a commander. Xenophon’s account of his early status matters when we later follow his assumption of responsibility."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "What a title does and does not establish",
        "body": [
          "The reading distinguishes στρατηγός, general, from λοχαγός, commander of a company, and στρατιώτης, soldier. These are roles in an army’s hierarchy, not degrees of personal worth. Xenophon says he began as none of the three. The reconstructed camp scene lets readers notice guards, equipment, food, and preparation without inventing a formal military appointment for him.",
          "The soldiers do not all possess the same information as Cyrus. Xenophon later says he himself was mistaken about the expedition’s objective. Reading a retrospective account requires attention to who knew what at a given moment. The Greek commanders’ recruitment and the author’s later explanation cannot be compressed into complete knowledge for everyone at Sardis."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The shepherd comparison",
        "body": [
          "In Memorabilia 3.2 Socrates discusses leadership with a man elected general and examines Homer’s image of a shepherd of the people. Care includes safety and supplies as well as military success. The leader’s excellence is judged partly by the condition of the people entrusted to him. The analogy does not erase the difference between human beings and sheep; it asks what responsibility a position entails.",
          "This companion scene comes from another work and another setting. We use it to evaluate the expedition, not to claim that Xenophon witnessed or recalled the conversation at Sardis. A reflection question follows: if friendship brings someone close to power, what must he learn before he can care responsibly for people affected by that power?"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "How is the Greek army at Sardis organized?",
        "answer": "It contains contingents recruited under different commanders."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What stated target does Cyrus offer?",
        "answer": "The Pisidians; the wider objective is not yet openly announced to the Greeks."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What is Xenophon’s initial military office?",
        "answer": "He says he is neither general, captain, nor ordinary soldier."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Does friendship with Proxenus establish command authority?",
        "answer": "No. It explains access and participation, not a formal appointment."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What does the shepherd comparison emphasize?",
        "answer": "A leader should care for the safety, supplies, and welfare of those entrusted to him."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why is the Socratic passage separate?",
        "answer": "It is a comparison from Memorabilia, not an attested camp conversation or memory."
      }
    ],
