    "translation": "In Athens Nicomachides comes from the elections. Socrates sees him and asks: “Who is general? What do the citizens say?”\n\nNicomachides says: “Antisthenes is general. But I have experience of military work. I have many wounds on my body.” And he shows the wounds to Socrates.\n\n“Antisthenes,” he says, “collects money. Is some merchant capable of commanding soldiers?” Socrates replies: “A general also provides food and money.”\n\nSocrates asks again: “Who prepares the choruses well? Antisthenes seeks experienced people and assigns the tasks to them.” Nicomachides says: “But a chorus is not an army.”\n\n“Whose task,” Socrates says, “is it to seek capable men and provide what is useful? The prudent leader does not do everything alone. He chooses good helpers.”\n\nNicomachides listens. Socrates says: “The guardian of a household and the leader of an army care for people. They both seek friends and protect resources. Not only effort, but also judgment is useful.”\n\nAnother account of Socrates also concerns preparation. The general provides food and weapons, organizes the soldiers, and examines the route. It is not enough to know only the formation.\n\nNow we examine Cyrus’s army again. A guide shows the soldiers a route; a prudent general cares for the soldiers. What does the army need? Who is capable of leading?",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Memorabilia 3.4.1–12. https://monadnock.net/xenophon/mem3-4.html\nXenophon, Memorabilia 3.1.1–7. https://monadnock.net/xenophon/mem3-1.html",
    "notesMarkdown": "Source and adaptation boundaries are stated in the introduction. All Greek is course-adapted present-tense narrative, not a verbatim ancient extract. Blue glosses support forms beyond the taught grammar; those untaught constructions are not assessed as morphology or production targets."
  },
