  "culture": {
    "title": "Elections, Choruses, and Practical Leadership",
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "A disappointed candidate",
        "body": [
          "Memorabilia 3.4 opens with Nicomachides returning from elections. He displays old wounds and points to his military service while complaining about the choice of Antisthenes. Xenophon uses this disagreement to examine what voters and candidates might count as qualifications. The passage does not tell us that visible scars are irrelevant; it questions whether they settle the whole issue.",
          "Antisthenes here is the elected general discussed by Nicomachides and Socrates. A shared name does not establish that he is the Socratic philosopher known from other sources. The adaptation keeps the identification limited. It also keeps this scene in Athens, separate from the expedition, without claiming Xenophon heard the exchange himself."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Why a chorus enters the argument",
        "body": [
          "Nicomachides contrasts organizing a chorus with organizing troops. Socrates observes that Antisthenes has selected skilled specialists and successfully managed performances. The civic chorus is not a military unit, but both activities involve assigning work, securing resources, and directing people toward a shared result. In the passage, Antisthenes’s experience as a chorēgos supplies the comparison.",
          "The analogy has limits that Nicomachides presses: actual fighting differs from performance. Socrates responds by considering practical preparation and readiness. We should evaluate this as an argument about transferable abilities, not a claim that theatrical success automatically certifies tactical expertise. The question for Cyrus’s army is which useful abilities it needs together."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Preparation and the people being led",
        "body": [
          "Memorabilia 3.1 criticizes instruction that reduces generalship to arranging formations. The discussion includes equipment, supplies, persistence, and the qualities needed to lead. Our short companion paragraph retains that breadth. Preparation is material work as well as a mental attitude; an army cannot survive on a confident speech alone.",
          "Memorabilia 3.4 also compares household management and public leadership in their dealings with people. Choosing competent helpers and obtaining cooperation matter. The comparison raises an ethical question: how should a leader treat the people whose work makes success possible? It does not give us license to erase differences between a household, a city, and an army."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "Why does Nicomachides display wounds?",
        "answer": "To support his claim that military experience qualifies him."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Who is Antisthenes in this passage?",
        "answer": "The elected general; the text used here does not establish that he is the Socratic philosopher."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why discuss chorus organization?",
        "answer": "It supplies an analogy for choosing skilled helpers and providing resources."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What limitation does Nicomachides press?",
        "answer": "Organizing a performance is different from fighting a battle."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What does preparation include beyond formation?",
        "answer": "Supplies, equipment, skilled people, and readiness to act."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "How might the comparison apply to Cyrus’s army?",
        "answer": "The army needs practical competence and care for people as well as military experience."
      }
    ],
    "review": {
      "title": "Before the Final Quiz",
      "items": [
        "Nasal ν drops before -σι; labials combine with σ as ψ; ρ remains in ῥήτορσι.",
        "σώφρων serves masculine/feminine; σῶφρον is neuter; all forms agree with their nouns.",
        "Accented τίς, τί asks a question; case identifies the role being questioned.",
        "Enclitic τις, τι leaves a person or thing unspecified and can affect the preceding word’s accent.",
        "What does preparation include beyond formation? Supplies, equipment, skilled people, and readiness to act.",
        "How might the comparison apply to Cyrus’s army? The army needs practical competence and care for people as well as military experience."
      ]
    },
