  "culture": {
    "title": "Marching, Royal Parks, and Overdue Wages",
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Reading an itinerary as human movement",
        "body": [
          "Anabasis 1.2.5–11 records stages, distances, stopping places, and the arrival of further forces. The boat bridge over the Maeander is an example of practical infrastructure that lets an army move. Our reading selects the crossing and a few stopping places to make the journey manageable rather than asking beginners to reproduce the full numerical itinerary.",
          "The road runs through Lydia into Phrygia. These are named regions in Xenophon’s account, not interchangeable labels for all of Asia. Travel joins geography to material tasks: carrying equipment, watching possessions, and arranging food. The ordinary conversations in our reading are reconstructed examples, while the named route and bridge are source-based."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "A park and a festival on campaign",
        "body": [
          "At Celaenae Xenophon describes Cyrus’s palace and a large hunting park, with the Maeander flowing through it. Additional contingents gather, and Cyrus reviews the Greeks there. The park belongs to the royal environment of the march; παράδεισος here does not mean an afterlife paradise. Geography, royal resources, and military assembly meet at this stop.",
          "At Peltae Xenias celebrates a festival for Lycaean Zeus with sacrifice and athletic games. Cyrus watches, and golden strigils are awarded as prizes. A strigil is the curved implement used to scrape oil and dirt from the body. The festival brings familiar Greek athletic and religious activity into the expedition without making the journey itself a holiday."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Pay as a condition of cooperation",
        "body": [
          "By Caystru-pedion the troops are owed more than three months’ wages. Xenophon reports repeated requests for payment and Cyrus’s expressions of hope. The amount in arrears matters: these men depend on resources, and promises alone do not remove their needs. Their pressure is part of the expedition’s practical story.",
          "Our distinction between μισθός, pay, and ἆθλον, prize, helps avoid treating rewards at games as a substitute for wages. The next lesson follows Epyaxa’s arrival and the payment of four months’ wages. A question for reflection is how a leader can secure cooperation when the material obligations that support it remain unmet."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "What practical feature helps the army cross the Maeander?",
        "answer": "A bridge made of boats."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What does παράδεισος mean at Celaenae?",
        "answer": "Cyrus’s royal hunting park."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Who organizes the Peltae festival?",
        "answer": "Xenias, in honor of Lycaean Zeus."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What are the prizes at the games?",
        "answer": "Golden strigils."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "How much pay is overdue at Caystru-pedion?",
        "answer": "More than three months’ pay."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why distinguish a prize from pay?",
        "answer": "Athletic prizes do not satisfy the army’s ordinary wage obligations."
      }
    ],
