    "translation": "Cyrus owes the pay, and the soldiers repeatedly request the money. Then Epyaxa comes to Cyrus. The wife of Syennesis, the ruler of the Cilicians, travels with guards.\n\nIt is reported that Epyaxa provides Cyrus with much money. At that time Cyrus gives the army four months’ pay. The gift of money is a report, whereas the four months’ pay is stated clearly in the account.\n\nThe army travels to Tyriaeum and stays there for three days. It is reported that Epyaxa wants to see the army. Cyrus draws up the whole army on the plain.\n\nThe Greeks have their weapons. Every hoplite carries a shield, and the generals arrange their own contingents. Cyrus and Epyaxa observe the formation from vehicles.\n\nThe trumpet signals, and all the Greeks run toward the camp. Epyaxa is frightened and flees in her carriage. The people in the market leave their goods behind and flee.\n\nThe soldiers laugh, but the traders are afraid. The soldiers’ hands carry weapons, while the traders’ hands no longer hold their belongings. The same display appears impressive to some and frightening to others.\n\nLater Epyaxa arrives at Tarsus before Cyrus. The army disrupts Cilicia, and the inhabitants are afraid. Syennesis does not approach Cyrus; but his wife persuades her husband. Syennesis receives assurances.\n\nSyennesis and Cyrus come to discussions. One provides money for the army; the other gives gifts and makes promises concerning the country. Epyaxa does more than watch: she helps bring about the meeting. The account does not reveal the woman’s private intention.",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 1.2.12–18, 25–27. https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Aabo%3Atlg%2C0032%2C006%3A1%3A2",
    "notesMarkdown": "Source and adaptation boundaries are stated in the introduction. All Greek is course-adapted present-tense narrative, not a verbatim ancient extract. Blue glosses support forms beyond the taught grammar; those untaught constructions are not assessed as morphology or production targets."
  },
