  "culture": {
    "title": "Epyaxa, Finance, and Civilian Fear",
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Resources and the status of a report",
        "body": [
          "Anabasis 1.2.12 names Epyaxa as the wife of Syennesis, ruler of the Cilicians. Her arrival occurs while Cyrus owes the soldiers pay. Xenophon reports a story that she gave him substantial money and then states the payment of four months’ wages. The payment is firmer within his narrative than the reported explanation of where the money came from.",
          "This distinction is a feature of the evidence, not a reason to remove Epyaxa from the story. Her presence and subsequent actions link finance with political relationships. Our adaptation avoids assigning her a speech or a hidden motive. It also avoids turning a reported financial transfer into independent proof about the exact origin of every coin paid to the troops."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The army on display and the market in flight",
        "body": [
          "At Tyriaeum Xenophon says that Epyaxa reportedly requested a review. Cyrus organizes the troops for display. The Greeks advance and then run toward their camp with a shout. Epyaxa flees in her vehicle, and people in the market abandon their wares. The soldiers’ laughter and Cyrus’s pleasure in the fear are part of the source’s uneven distribution of experience.",
          "The review is impressive military coordination, but the same event terrifies people outside the formation. A market’s goods and the traders depending on them belong to the material story of war. Our adapted contrast of hands carrying weapons and hands losing goods makes this difference visible; that specific hand description is educational reconstruction."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Negotiation and Cilicia’s vulnerability",
        "body": [
          "The compressed continuation turns to Tarsus. Anabasis 1.2.25–27 records losses among Menon’s men, plundering in anger, and Syennesis’s reluctance to meet a more powerful figure. Epyaxa helps persuade her husband, and assurances are required before he meets Cyrus. The exact motives behind her decisions are not preserved in the account used here.",
          "At the meeting Syennesis supplies money, and Cyrus gives prestigious gifts and promises concerning the land and captives. This follows harm to Cilicia; it is not a peaceful scene isolated from the army’s actions. Epyaxa acts within the political process, while civilians remain exposed to military pressure. Reflection should consider whose security a successful negotiation protects and whose losses remain."
        ]
      }
    ],
