    "translation": "Socrates, Laches, and Nicias discuss courage. Socrates asks, “What is courage?” Laches listens and considers the soldiers in battle.\n\nLaches says, “The man remaining in his position and fighting against the enemies is brave. He does not flee but saves his friends.” Socrates watches the speaker.\n\nSocrates asks, “And what of those fleeing? The Scythians fight both while fleeing and while pursuing. The retreating horsemen are not all cowardly.” Laches agrees about the horsemen.\n\nSocrates also speaks about the Spartans: “The retreating soldiers fight again.” Laches accepts the example. For not only those remaining but also those retreating appear brave.\n\nSocrates does not ask only about war. “The person bearing illness, the person living in poverty, and the person speaking in the city often face dangers. What do brave people have in common?”\n\nLaches speaks again: “Courage is a kind of endurance.” Socrates asks, “Is all endurance good? Endurance with judgment is good; but what of endurance without judgment?” The listeners consider the question.\n\nThe discussion still proceeds; those conversing do not stop investigating. Laches admires the man remaining in his position, while Socrates also examines his judgment. Does the person persisting always act rightly?\n",
    "sourceCitation": "Plato, Laches 190e–192d. Controlled course adaptation; dialogue and teaching futures are newly supplied, not verbatim quotations. https://www.platonicfoundation.org/translation/platos-laches/ https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1584",
    "notesMarkdown": "Course adaptation of Plato, Laches 190e–192d. The initial definition, fighting during retreat, courage outside battle, and the question about endurance follow the passage. All Greek dialogue below is newly simplified course wording; the frame and transitions are supplied. This selection does not solve the whole dialogue’s inquiry."
