      {
        "title": "An example is not a complete definition",
        "body": [
          "Plato frames this discussion around the education of young men and the value of military training. Laches and Nicias have military standing, while Socrates tests what their claims actually cover. The selection begins with Laches’s familiar soldier who holds his place. That is intelligible as an example of courage, but an example need not include every courageous act.",
          "Socrates does not simply declare the example false. He accepts that such a soldier can be brave and asks whether courage also appears elsewhere. This is a model of critical inquiry: a counterexample tests the breadth of a claim. The course frame places the three named speakers in Plato’s conversation; it does not insert Xenophon into the cast."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Retreat and the purpose of movement",
        "body": [
          "At Laches 191a–c Socrates mentions Scythian horsemen and a reported Spartan maneuver at Plataea. Fighting during withdrawal or turning after retreat challenges the claim that staying physically still is necessary for courage. The reading summarizes those examples in present Greek instead of reproducing Plato’s complex narrative.",
          "This tactical comparison matters to Cyrus’s expedition because movement away from danger will later be unavoidable. It does not mean any retreat is wise. The source offers cases to examine, and the learner must ask what a movement accomplishes, who benefits, and whether the people making it have good reasons."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Endurance and judgment",
        "body": [
          "At 191d Socrates extends the inquiry to danger at sea, illness, poverty, and civic affairs, as well as fears and desires. The lesson selects illness, poverty, and public speaking to keep the Greek manageable. Courage becomes a question about facing difficulty across several parts of life.",
          "Laches’s next proposal makes courage a kind of endurance. By 192d Socrates is distinguishing endurance with judgment from endurance without it. The dialogue continues beyond this excerpt. Our closing question invites comparison with leaders and soldiers; it does not announce a definition that Plato’s whole discussion has conclusively established."
        ]
      }
    ],
