    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "A changed risk and the right to question it",
        "body": [
          "At Anabasis 1.3.1 the soldiers refuse to march further because they suspect a campaign against the King and say that this was not what they were hired to do. Their objection concerns the scope of the service and its danger. The source gives grounds for interpreting their refusal as a claim about agreement rather than automatic cowardice.",
          "Clearchus tries force and faces stones aimed at him and his pack animals. The violence is serious, and Xenophon says he nearly dies. Acknowledging the soldiers’ legitimate concern does not require approving every method they use. The episode asks how a commander secures cooperation when the original understanding has become doubtful."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Public loyalty and private persuasion",
        "body": [
          "Clearchus weeps before an assembly and declares solidarity with his men. Soldiers from other contingents join him. Yet he privately reassures Cyrus and arranges for Cyrus to keep summoning him while he publicly refuses. Xenophon presents conduct that can be read as both concern for his troops and calculated management of the crisis.",
          "The army’s assembly is a place for speeches and collective deliberation, but it is not simply the Athenian democratic Assembly transported into Asia. Clearchus encourages some speakers, and the soldiers depend on Cyrus’s camp for supplies. The reading preserves the tension between speaking openly and shaping what choices seem possible."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Leaving also requires resources",
        "body": [
          "Speakers discuss vessels, a guide, food, safe routes, and the danger of trusting Cyrus after opposing him. The source also notes harm already done to Cilicians: one speaker fears that people whose property the soldiers have taken could block their departure. The difficulty of going home is logistical and political, not just a lack of resolve.",
          "The negotiated explanation names Abrocomas on the Euphrates. Cyrus promises monthly pay of one and a half darics instead of one, and the troops continue while still suspecting the King as the target. Anabasis 1.3.21 explicitly says that no public announcement of that target occurs here. Additional pay settles the immediate movement without removing the information problem."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "Why is the refusal more than simple disobedience?",
        "answer": "The soldiers question a danger and objective they say were outside the service for which they were hired."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What changes after force fails?",
        "answer": "Clearchus turns to speeches, private communication, and negotiation."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why is his conduct ambiguous?",
        "answer": "He publicly aligns with the soldiers while privately assuring Cyrus that the situation can be managed."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Why is leaving difficult?",
        "answer": "Routes, guides, vessels, supplies, Cyrus’s power, and hostile people create risks."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What change in pay is promised?",
        "answer": "Monthly pay rises from one daric to one and a half darics."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Is war against the King publicly announced at Tarsus?",
        "answer": "No. Suspicion remains; explicit announcement comes later at Thapsacus."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Which leadership decision in this lesson needs reasons beyond personal bravery?",
        "answer": "Securing cooperation requires addressing the agreed service and information, not merely demanding obedience."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "How can the reading’s source boundary improve your interpretation?",
        "answer": "Distinguish the author’s reported events and speeches from the course’s simplified wording, supplied examples, and ethical inferences."
      }
    ],
