    "translation": "Cyrus and the Greeks come to Thapsacus. The city is beside the Euphrates River. Here Cyrus says openly, “We shall march against the Great King.”\n\nThe generals report Cyrus’s statement to the soldiers. They are angry: “Now we learn of the war. We shall not follow without money.” So they request money for the long journey.\n\nCyrus promises five minas to each soldier at Babylon. “I shall give you money, and I shall provide pay until the arrival back in Ionia.” The soldiers hear the promise.\n\nThe Greeks cross the Euphrates. The people of the city marvel at the crossing; the water is not deep. The army now advances, and the war against the King is openly known.\n\nIn another place, a short time before the battle, Cyrus summons the generals and captains. He says, “Men of Greece, I honor you and admire your freedom. Now we shall deliberate about the battle.”\n\n“The enemies are numerous and will fight with much shouting. But you will take courage. Upon victory I shall make my friends wealthy and give golden crowns.” In this way Cyrus encourages his listeners.\n\nSome rejoice, while others ask about the promises: “Will Cyrus provide the money? What will each person receive?” The generals urge him not to fight but to remain behind. Victory is not yet certain.\n",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 1.4.11–18 and 1.7.3–9. Controlled course adaptation; dialogue and teaching futures are newly supplied, not verbatim quotations. https://www.mikrosapoplous.gr/xenophon/anb3d10.htm https://www.mikrosapoplous.gr/xenophon/anb7d10.htm",
    "notesMarkdown": "Course adaptation of Xenophon, Anabasis 1.4.11–18 and 1.7.3–9. The announced objective, demand for compensation, five-mina promise, continuing pay, crossing, and later encouragement follow the sources. The simplified future speeches are new course wording, not quotations. Promises about wealth and victory are the speakers’ expectations, not the narrator’s guarantees."
