    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "Sigmatic Futures: Stem, Sigma, and Endings: The regular future adds σ to a verb stem and uses primary endings. λύω becomes λύσω, “I shall release”; λυόμεθα becomes λυσόμεθα, “we shall release for ourselves.” There is no augment: future forms are not past-tense forms.",
        "Contract Futures of Verbs in -ίζω: Many Attic verbs in -ίζω form an asigmatic contracted future. The present νομίζω gives future νομιῶ, νομιεῖς, νομιεῖ; plural νομιοῦμεν, νομιεῖτε, νομιοῦσι(ν). In the surface form there is no sigma: the stem contracts with future endings in -εω.",
        "Sigmatic Futures of Contract Verbs: Present contract verbs normally lengthen their stem vowel before future sigma. τιμάω gives τιμήσω; ποιέω gives ποιήσω; δηλόω gives δηλώσω. These are sigmatic futures even though their present verbs contract.",
        "Deponents and Futures with Middle Form: A future can have middle endings and an active English meaning. πορεύομαι → πορεύσομαι, “I shall travel”; βουλεύομαι → βουλεύσομαι, “I shall deliberate.” These verbs already have middle forms in the present. Learn the future with the dictionary entry.",
        "Prediction, Intention, and Promise: A future indicative places an action in expectation, but its practical force depends on context. πορευσόμεθα announces what Cyrus intends the expedition to do. ποιήσω promises what he undertakes to provide. μαχοῦνται predicts what the enemies will do."
