        "title": "Disclosure at Thapsacus",
        "body": [
          "At Anabasis 1.4.11 Cyrus tells the Greek commanders that the march is against the Great King and asks them to relay it. The soldiers are angry with their generals and say that they had known the objective but concealed it. Xenophon reports their accusation; the course does not convert that accusation into proof of what every commander knew.",
          "The soldiers demand an offer comparable to what they expected from earlier service. Cyrus promises five minas of silver to each upon reaching Babylon and full pay until he brings the Greeks back to Ionia. They accept and cross. The chronological contrast with Tarsus is essential: suspicion there becomes explicit disclosure here."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Crossing and reported interpretation",
        "body": [
          "Xenophon says the Euphrates can be forded at this crossing. The people of Thapsacus report that previously it had not been passable on foot and interpret its apparent submission to Cyrus as a sign that he should rule. These are the inhabitants’ reported words and interpretation, not a verified miracle or a hydrological rule for every year.",
          "The adapted Greek retains the shallow water and their astonishment while omitting complex supernatural claims. Crossing the river changes the army’s practical situation, but the reading does not yet give the outcome of the royal contest. A source can record what people regard as a sign without endorsing that sign as a guarantee."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Persuasion shortly before the battle",
        "body": [
          "The second source scene, Anabasis 1.7.3–9, takes place later, near the approaching battle. Cyrus praises the Greeks and their freedom, describes the enemies’ numbers and noise, and offers rewards. His comparative claims about peoples are persuasive claims by a commander, not objective rankings of human worth.",
          "Gaulites reports doubts about whether Cyrus will remember or be able to fulfill his offers. Cyrus answers with the resources of the empire and promises golden crowns; those speaking with him also urge him to avoid fighting personally and to remain behind them. The army’s encouragement coexists with uncertainty about the patron and his choices."
        ]
      }
