      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "Liquid and Nasal Futures: Many verbs whose stems end in λ, μ, ν, or ρ form an asigmatic contracted future. The future stem combines with endings in -εω and contracts: μένω → μενῶ, “I shall remain”; ἀγγέλλω → ἀγγελῶ, “I shall announce.” The latter future uses a single λ.",
        "εἶμι and εἰμί: εἶμι means “I go” and in ordinary Attic usage often carries future meaning, “I shall go.” Its indicative forms are εἶμι, εἶ, εἶσι(ν); ἴμεν, ἴτε, ἴασι(ν). Its infinitive is ἰέναι, “to go.” This is a supplied irregular paradigm, not a regular sigmatic future.",
        "Future Participles Expressing Purpose: A future active participle can express the purpose of coming or going: ἔρχεται ἐρωτήσων, “he comes to ask.” The asking is intended after or as the goal of the approach, not an ongoing action simultaneous with it. Compare present ἐρωτῶν, “asking.”",
        "δεῖ and χρή with an Infinitive: δεῖ means “it is necessary” and χρή means “one must” or “it is fitting.” They are impersonal: the Greek form does not have a personal subject meaning “I” or “you.” An infinitive names the required action: δεῖ σῴζειν, “it is necessary to save.”",
        "Direct Questions and Question Words: A direct question can ask for a fact or explanation: τίς; “who?”, τί; “what?” or “why?” in context, ποῦ; “where?”, πότε; “when?”, and διὰ τί; “why?” The Greek question mark is the semicolon. A question can use present or future forms."
