    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "A dated scene in a divided city",
        "body": [
          "This comparison takes place in 403 BCE, after the fighting at Munychia, before the expedition of 401. Athens is divided between the Thirty’s regime and its opponents. Hellenica 2.4.19 describes recovery of the dead and conversation between men from the opposing sides before Cleocritus speaks.",
          "The fighting at Munychia and the subsequent appeal belong to 403 BCE, during the struggle to end the Thirty’s rule. The Greek reading retells the scene in the present for learners and supplies no claim that Xenophon attended. Military victory has created an opportunity to speak, not a city already reconciled."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The herald’s appeal to a shared life",
        "body": [
          "Cleocritus is identified as the herald of the initiates and as having a fine voice. He addresses the others as citizens, asking why they drive out and seek to kill people with whom they have shared rites, sacrifices, festivals, dancing, education, and military dangers. The speech relies on relationships that cross the current political divide.",
          "He adds family and marriage connections, companionship, common safety and freedom, and grief even for the other side’s dead. The adaptation selects these ties and turns the remembered shared experience into present descriptions. They are reasons offered to change allegiance and action; they are not evidence that every individual on each side responds alike."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "An appeal interrupted, and an obligation examined",
        "body": [
          "Cleocritus urges his hearers to stop wronging the homeland and to reject the Thirty. His accusation that the rulers kill for private gain belongs to the speech Xenophon reports. At 2.4.22 the opposing officials, after hearing this, lead their men away. The passage does not report immediate reconciliation produced by the appeal.",
          "The civic comparison asks what leadership is for. Cyrus offers benefits tied to his royal war; Cleocritus appeals to shared ties and safety among fellow citizens. The future intentions and purpose participles in the Greek are explicitly course additions. The learner can compare competing obligations while keeping distinct the speech, the officials’ response, and the still unresolved conflict."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "How does this scene fit the module chronology?",
        "answer": "It is Athens in 403 BCE, a separate comparison before Cyrus’s 401 expedition."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What makes Cleocritus’s voice matter?",
        "answer": "As a herald with a fine voice he can gain attention across the opposing groups."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Which shared ties does he invoke?",
        "answer": "Religious rites, festivals, education, military service, kinship, marriage ties, companionship, and grief."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What is the immediate official response?",
        "answer": "The opposing officials lead their men away; the speech does not instantly reunite Athens."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What are the future-purpose statements in the Greek?",
        "answer": "Labeled teaching adaptations, not quotations preserved in Hellenica."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "How does the appeal differ from Cyrus’s promises?",
        "answer": "It stresses common civic safety and relationships rather than rewards for advancing a ruler’s campaign."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Which leadership decision in this lesson needs reasons beyond personal bravery?",
        "answer": "Calling citizens to reject destructive orders needs a purpose grounded in common safety."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "How can the reading’s source boundary improve your interpretation?",
        "answer": "Distinguish the author’s reported events and speeches from the course’s simplified wording, supplied examples, and ethical inferences."
      }
    ],
