    "translation": "The Greeks advanced toward their opponents. The opponents fled; the Greeks pursued them, but told one another to keep their formation.\n\nThe Greeks defeated the opponents in front of them; Cyrus, however, sought his brother. The Greeks’ victory did not decide the whole battle.\n\nCyrus saw the king and rushed toward him. A few friends went with him. There Cyrus fell and died.\n\nThe Greeks did not see Cyrus dying. They considered the victory in their own sector great. They had not yet learned what had happened to Cyrus.\n\nOn the next day messengers came to the Greeks. They said Cyrus was dead. On hearing the report, the soldiers learned of the disaster.\n\nThe Greeks took counsel about survival. They won the struggle in their own sector, but lost Cyrus. Now they must seek a route toward Greece.",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 1.8.17–29; 2.1.1–4. https://cais-soas.com/CAIS/History/hakhamaneshian/xenophon/anabasis/xeno_anbs_book_1.htm https://cais-soas.com/CAIS/History/hakhamaneshian/xenophon/anabasis/xeno_anbs_book_2.htm",
    "notesMarkdown": "The simplified Greek is newly composed for this course. It preserves the source distinctions explained above while reducing sentence complexity. The English translation aligns paragraph for paragraph. Supplementary grammar examples and commands illustrate forms and are not additional historical claims."
  },
  "wordStudy": {
    "label": "Word Study — Victory, report, and survival",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "title": "Victory, report, and survival",
        "practiceTopic": "word-study",
        "body": [
          "νίκη means victory, while συμφορά means disaster. In this lesson both words describe the same battlefield at different scales. The possessive phrase ἡ τῶν Ἑλλήνων νίκη identifies whose local success is meant; it does not assert Cyrus’s accession.",
          "ἄγγελος is a messenger and ἀγγελία a report. Follow the message from arrival to learning: ἦλθον, then ἔμαθον. These whole-event forms move the narrative through a change in knowledge.",
          "σωτηρία, survival or deliverance, becomes the army’s new concern. βουλὴν λαμβάνειν means to take counsel: the reading uses the aorist βουλὴν ἔλαβον. Distinguish a verb’s lexical meaning from the event’s effect on the story."
        ],
        "display": [
          {
            "greek": "νίκη",
            "english": "victory in the Greek sector"
          },
          {
            "greek": "συμφορά",
            "english": "disaster for the expedition"
          },
          {
            "greek": "ἄγγελος / ἀγγελία",
            "english": "messenger / report"
          },
          {
            "greek": "βουλὴν ἔλαβον",
            "english": "they took counsel"
          },
          {
            "greek": "σωτηρία",
            "english": "survival"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
