    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "Aorist indicative in past narrative: The indicative aorist normally locates an event in past time. An augment precedes the verbal stem, and secondary endings mark person and number. In ἔλαβον, ἐ- is the syllabic augment, λαβ- is the aorist stem, and -ον is an ending.",
        "Thematic second aorist: active and middle: Thematic second aorists put ο or ε between their stem and their endings. The active pattern is ἔλαβον, ἔλαβες, ἔλαβε(ν), ἐλάβομεν, ἐλάβετε, ἔλαβον. Notice accent movement when another syllable is added.",
        "Aspect: action viewed as a whole: Aspect is the way a speaker presents an action. A present stem commonly opens an action from inside, as ongoing or repeated. An aorist stem presents the action as a whole without focusing on its internal unfolding.",
        "Active and middle aorist participles: The active second-aorist participle uses the stem without augment: λαβών, λαβοῦσα, λαβόν. Its masculine/neuter stem contains -οντ-; the feminine has -ουσα. Like an adjective, it agrees in case, number, and gender with the person or thing described.",
        "Common second aorists and principal parts: Learn the present, future, and aorist together as the first three principal parts. They identify stems needed for different systems. λαμβάνω, λήψομαι, ἔλαβον cannot be predicted by merely appending an ending to λαμβαν-."
      ]
    }
