  "culture": {
    "title": "Culture and History",
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Two scales of victory",
        "body": [
          "A battle can contain successes and failures in different sectors. The Greeks drive back the troops facing them, while Cyrus’s attack brings him into direct danger near the king. The course therefore uses “Greek victory” only for the local encounter.",
          "The expedition sought to replace Artaxerxes with Cyrus. Cyrus’s death removes that purpose even though the Greeks retain military strength. Evaluating leadership requires connecting a successful action to the aim it was meant to serve."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Information reaches an army unevenly",
        "body": [
          "The narrator can tell readers about Cyrus’s death before the Greek soldiers know it. This distinction matters when we judge their confidence after the battle. Their limited view of the field is part of the episode, not proof that they knew and ignored the disaster.",
          "Messages turn that limited view into a new practical problem: survival without the expedition’s royal patron. Read the sequence of actions carefully. A reader should neither grant the soldiers the narrator’s knowledge too early nor mistake their local success for the outcome of the campaign."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Bravery and responsibility",
        "body": [
          "Cyrus’s personal attack can be described as bold while its consequences remain disastrous. Courage does not alone guarantee a sound decision. The reading leaves room to examine what a leader owes the many people who depend on his survival.",
          "Compare this military aim with Cleocritus’s civic appeal in Lesson 20. One seeks a throne; the other urges citizens to recognize shared obligations after civil violence. The comparison evaluates purposes and consequences without pretending that the two speeches occurred together."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "Why is the local victory insufficient?",
        "answer": "Cyrus’s death defeats the expedition’s aim. Their purpose was to establish Cyrus, not simply win one encounter."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Whose knowledge is broader at first?",
        "answer": "the narrator’s. The narrator reports events away from the Greek sector."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What makes leadership judgment more than praise for boldness?",
        "answer": "attention to purposes and consequences. The culture page links decisions to the people affected."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Which purpose belongs to Cleocritus’s comparison?",
        "answer": "shared civic obligations. Lesson 20 concerns fellow citizens in Athens."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What changes after the report?",
        "answer": "the practical aim becomes survival. Cyrus’s death forces a new aim."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What should a reader avoid?",
        "answer": "giving soldiers knowledge they did not yet have. Sequence and viewpoint prevent false inferences."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Which decision best tests responsibility in this reading? Explain with an action and consequence.",
        "answer": "Responses may differ; use the reading’s specific decisions and the source distinctions to support your judgment."
      },
