    "translation": "After Cyrus’s death, the Greeks entered negotiations with Tissaphernes. Clearchus spoke about the route and trust. Tissaphernes invited the generals.\n\nSome of the soldiers saw the danger. They said to Clearchus: “All the leaders must not go together.” Clearchus, however, trusted Tissaphernes.\n\nFive generals and twenty captains came to the doors. The generals entered, while the captains remained outside. Socrates the Achaean went with the generals; this man is not the Athenian philosopher.\n\nThe captains remained at the doors. The enemies seized the generals and killed the captains. Xenophon speaks elsewhere about the generals’ later deaths.\n\nThe soldiers in camp saw the horsemen. They did not learn what had happened immediately. Nicarchus came to them wounded and told the news.\n\nOn hearing the news, the Greeks ran to their arms. The generals are no longer in camp, but the soldiers still have weapons. They must take new counsel.",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 2.5.25–35; 2.6 for the generals’ later fates. https://cais-soas.com/CAIS/History/hakhamaneshian/xenophon/anabasis/xeno_anbs_book_2.htm",
