  "culture": {
    "title": "Culture and History",
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "Oaths, negotiations, and risk",
        "body": [
          "Anabasis presents the negotiations against a background of oaths and mutual suspicion. Clearchus treats the proposed meeting as a way to identify those spreading accusations. Trust in an agreement can have practical value, but the text also records soldiers who object.",
          "Those objections concern concentrating the leaders in one place and trusting Tissaphernes. A leader’s confidence is therefore not the only voice available in the source. Ask what information and safeguards a decision needs, without claiming that the recorded objectors predicted every later event."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Generals and captains are different offices",
        "body": [
          "A στρατηγός is a general; a λοχαγός commands a company. At the meeting five generals enter while twenty captains wait outside. The simultaneous attack gives the two groups different immediate fates: seizure within, killing outside.",
          "The capture removes important commanders but does not physically dissolve the Greek army. The camp still contains soldiers, arms, and surviving officers. Socrates the Achaean belongs to this military group; he must never be confused with the philosopher Socrates of Athens."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Evidence and the order of events",
        "body": [
          "From camp, the Greeks first see alarming movements without knowing their cause. Nicarchus’s arrival supplies a report. The reading suppresses graphic details of his injury while preserving his role as a wounded survivor and messenger.",
          "Anabasis 2.6 discusses the captured generals’ later fates and character. That retrospective chapter must not be collapsed into an execution at the meeting’s doors. An accurate account can be compact while retaining the distinction between capture, immediate killings, and later death."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "questions": [
      {
        "prompt": "Why record the objections?",
        "answer": "they show dissent within the army. The source preserves more than Clearchus’s confidence."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What does λοχαγός mean here?",
        "answer": "company captain. A captain differs from a general."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "Which distinction protects the chronology?",
        "answer": "capture now versus later fates. The later retrospective chapter is a separate stage."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What remains after the betrayal?",
        "answer": "soldiers, arms, and surviving officers. The army must reorganize; it has not dissolved."
      },
      {
        "prompt": "What changes the camp’s information?",
        "answer": "a survivor’s report. Nicarchus reports what the watching soldiers did not know."
      },
      {
