    "translation": "During the night Xenophon slept a little. He saw a dream: fire appeared around his father’s house. On waking, he considered the dream.\n\nThe light gave him hope, but the fire gave him fear. Xenophon did not receive a certain promise of survival. He reasoned about the danger.\n\nThen he got up and summoned Proxenus’s captains. The captains came together. Xenophon began a speech to them.\n\nXenophon took thought for the soldiers. “We must prepare,” he said. After hearing and considering, the captains accepted the proposal.\n\nAfter summoning the other leaders too, the captains held a council. Many deliberated about survival. Xenophon did not act alone.\n\nThe officers chose new generals. Xenophon became a general in Proxenus’s place. Other men too took command: survival is a shared task.\n\nIn a different account Socrates questions Euthydemus about courage. A person ignorant of danger is not courageous for that reason. The courageous person must understand dangers.",
    "sourceCitation": "Xenophon, Anabasis 3.1.11–25, 32–47; separate companion, Memorabilia 4.6.10–11. https://cais-soas.com/CAIS/History/hakhamaneshian/xenophon/anabasis/xeno_anbs_book_3.htm https://monadnock.net/xenophon/mem4-6.html",
    "notesMarkdown": "The simplified Greek is newly composed for this course. It preserves the source distinctions explained above while reducing sentence complexity. The English translation aligns paragraph for paragraph. Supplementary grammar examples and commands illustrate forms and are not additional historical claims."
