    "summary": {
      "title": "Grammar Summary",
      "items": [
        "Sigmatic first aorist: active and middle: A regular sigmatic first aorist adds σ to the verbal stem, usually with α in the endings. λύω gives ἔλυσα, “I released,” and middle ἐλυσάμην. Past indicative augment is added before the stem just as in the second aorist.",
        "Consonant changes and vowel lengthening: Adding σ to a consonant stem produces regular spelling changes: labials π, β, φ + σ yield ψ; velars κ, γ, χ + σ yield ξ; dental sounds τ, δ, θ disappear before σ. Thus πέμπω → ἔπεμψα, πράττω → ἔπραξα, and πείθω → ἔπεισα.",
        "Active and middle first-aorist participles: The active first-aorist participle has λύσας, λύσασα, λῦσαν. It lacks augment. Its masculine and neuter stem contains -σαντ-: genitive λύσαντος. The feminine follows first-declension λύσασα, λυσάσης. Accent and agreement must be read together.",
        "First and second aorists share aorist aspect: “First” and “second” name formation patterns, not the order in which events occurred. First aorist ἔλυσα has σ and α-based endings; second aorist ἔλαβον has a thematic ο/ε pattern and its own stem. Both present an action as a whole."
      ]
    }
  },
  "culture": {
    "title": "Culture and History",
    "body": [],
    "sections": [
      {
        "title": "A dream with two possible readings",
        "body": [
          "Xenophon reports a dream of fire and describes both encouragement and anxiety in interpreting it. The light can suggest help, while the surrounding fire can suggest entrapment in the king’s territory. The narrative gives his uncertainty a place in the account.",
          "We should describe this as a reported dream and interpretation. It supplies neither an independently verified divine promise nor permission to erase his fear. His next acts are practical: summon officers, discuss the danger, and begin organizing a response."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Replacement command is collective",
        "body": [
          "Xenophon first calls Proxenus’s captains, then the consultation reaches surviving commanders from other units. Cheirisophus also speaks and presses for prompt elections. The source depicts a process involving several officers rather than one man assuming absolute control.",
          "Xenophon is chosen in Proxenus’s place; four other named replacements are selected for the other missing generals. “Xenophon became a general” is accurate. “Xenophon became the army’s sole leader” would distort the distributed command and its shared responsibilities."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Socrates and informed courage",
        "body": [
          "In the separate Memorabilia 4.6 discussion, Socrates questions Euthydemus about courage. Ignorance of danger does not make someone courageous simply by removing fear. Knowledge and sound response matter to the ethical definition being explored.",
          "This is a comparison between Xenophon’s works, not a claim that the officers studied a later book during the crisis. It helps us evaluate the expedition’s decisions: getting up and speaking are valuable when they begin informed action for others, not merely a performance of confidence."
        ]
      }
    ],
